<!doctype html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
  <meta charset="utf-8">
  <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1">
  <title>{% block title %}Ridal{% endblock %}</title>
  <link rel="icon" href="/favicon.ico" type="image/png">
  {# app.css must come after leaflet.css so our .leaflet-* overrides win. #}
  <link rel="stylesheet" href="/static/leaflet.css">
  <link rel="stylesheet" href="/static/app.css">
  {% block extrahead %}{% endblock %}
</head>
<body>
  <header>
    {#- Same reasoning as the menu links below: the logo is another way home,
        and it should not be the one that quietly resets the profile. -#}
    {%- set profile_query = "?profile=" ~ active_profile if active_profile else "" %}
    <a href="/{{ profile_query }}" class="brand">
      <img src="/static/images/logo.svg" alt="" class="brand-logo">
      Ridal
    </a>
    {#- A menu rather than a row of links: project settings, sign in/out and
        whatever else follows all belong here, and a header that grows a link
        per page stops working quickly on a phone. `<details>` gives a
        keyboard-accessible disclosure with no JavaScript; app.js only adds
        closing it on Escape or an outside click. -#}
    <nav class="site-nav">
      <details class="site-menu" id="site-menu">
        <summary aria-label="Menu">Menu</summary>
        {#- The render profile is a viewing preference, so it rides along
            through every page rather than resetting whenever the menu is
            used. `active_profile` is undefined on pages that have none (the
            error page), which minijinja treats as falsy -- so the links
            simply come out bare there. -#}
        <ul>
          <li><a href="/{{ profile_query }}">Radargram catalog</a></li>
          <li><a href="/layers{{ profile_query }}">Layers</a></li>
          <li><a href="/settings{{ profile_query }}">Settings</a></li>
          {#- The sign-in control is offered only where signing in can
              succeed. A project with no accounts has nothing to sign in to,
              and a link to a login page that always fails is worse than no
              link -- the same rule the picking toolbar follows. Both
              variables are undefined on pages that do not resolve a caller
              (the error page), which minijinja treats as falsy, so nothing
              is shown there rather than something wrong. -#}
          {% if authentication_configured %}
            {% if current_user %}
              <li class="site-menu-identity">
                Signed in as <strong>{{ current_user }}</strong>
                <span class="hint">{{ current_role }}</span>
              </li>
              <li><a href="/login">Sign out</a></li>
            {% else %}
              <li><a href="/login">Sign in</a></li>
            {% endif %}
          {% endif %}
        </ul>
      </details>
    </nav>
  </header>
  <main>
    {% block content %}{% endblock %}
  </main>
  {# app.js after leaflet.js, so both L and RIDAL exist before page scripts. #}
  <script src="/static/leaflet.js"></script>
  <script src="/static/app.js"></script>
  {% block extrascript %}{% endblock %}
</body>
</html>
